# Valgrind memcheck suppressions for the Free Pascal runtime library (FPC 3.2.2
# and 3.2.4, x86_64). Pass with --suppressions=scripts/valgrind/fpc-rtl.supp; the
# scheduled memory-safety job in .github/workflows/fuzz.yml does.
#
# IndexByte and IndexWord (rtl/x86_64/x86_64.inc) scan with aligned 16-byte
# SSE2 loads (movdqa), compare the whole block (pcmpeqb/pcmpeqw, pmovmskb) and
# branch on any match before they drop one at or past the length. Unless the
# buffer ends on a 16-byte boundary, the last load reads bytes past its end.
# The load stays inside one aligned 16-byte block, so it never crosses into
# another page and cannot fault, and those bytes never decide the result.
# Memcheck still reports the branch when they are undefined, for example the
# slack after a string's terminator in a block FPC's heap manager reused
# without telling Valgrind (#1776).
#
# What this gives up: each record matches every Memcheck:Cond report whose
# innermost frame is the routine, so an undefined length, an undefined search
# value or undefined bytes inside the searched range are hidden in these two
# routines as well. When the search finds nothing its "not found" result is a
# constant, so nothing undefined leaves the routine; when it matches undefined
# bytes the returned index is undefined and memcheck reports where a caller
# branches on it. Undefined bytes inside the range are reported again only where other
# code makes a branch, an address or a system call depend on them.
#
# The reasoning holds for the FPC 3.2.2 and 3.2.4 routines, whose source is the
# same; the job warns when it runs another version.
{
   fpc-rtl-indexbyte-aligned-overread
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:SYSTEM_$$_INDEXBYTE$*
}
{
   fpc-rtl-indexword-aligned-overread
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:SYSTEM_$$_INDEXWORD$*
}
